# ---- 3 stars
(3,'🟢',"Image, container and layer, explained for an ML engineer. A teammate says 'my model changed inside the container but it is gone after restart'. Explain what an image is, what a container adds on top (writable layer), how image layers are shared and read-only, what docker commit does and why you should not use it, and where files written at runtime go. Show the commands you would use to prove it (docker run, docker diff, docker history, docker rm), and mark clearly which were not executed here.",GEN),
(3,'🟡',"Layer caching on an ML inference service (an eBay-style question). A Dockerfile does COPY . /app first, then pip install -r requirements.txt, then downloads a 500 MB model, and every one-line code change triggers a 12 minute rebuild. Explain how the build cache decides to reuse a layer, rewrite the Dockerfile so a code change rebuilds in seconds, and say what still busts the cache (a changed requirements.txt, a timestamp or git-hash ARG placed early, apt-get update on its own line). Include a small Python script that simulates cache keys by hashing instruction plus file contents so you can show which layers rebuild.",PRA),
(3,'🟡',"Multi-stage builds for an ML service. Write a Dockerfile with a builder stage (compilers, build headers, wheels built or a virtualenv created) and a final runtime stage that copies only the virtualenv and the app. Explain what is left behind, how COPY --from works, how to target one stage with --target for tests, and what breaks if the runtime stage lacks a shared library the wheels need. Lint the Dockerfile with hadolint if it can be installed.",GEN),
(3,'🟡',"Choosing a base image for a Python ML service: python:3.x, python:3.x-slim, alpine, distroless and ubuntu. Explain glibc versus musl and why numpy, scipy, torch and many manylinux wheels are painful or slow on alpine, what slim leaves out (compilers, some libs), when distroless is worth it and how you debug it, and how you pick a tag (pin the minor version and OS release). Give your default choice for a scikit-learn model API and for a PyTorch service and the reasons.",GEN),
(3,'🟡',"CUDA images for deep learning. Explain the nvidia/cuda tag flavours (base, runtime, devel) and when you need each, why a PyTorch pip wheel already ships its own CUDA libraries so you often do not need a devel image, how to pick between nvidia/cuda, pytorch/pytorch and a plain python-slim image with the right wheel index, and how image size changes between them. Show a Dockerfile for a GPU inference service and say what cannot be tested without a GPU.",GPU),
(3,'🔴',"How does a container use the host GPU? Explain what the NVIDIA Container Toolkit installs and configures (runtime hook, nvidia-ctk, daemon.json), what docker run --gpus all does, which pieces come from the host (driver, libcuda) and which from the image (CUDA runtime, cuDNN), the rule that the container CUDA version may be older than the driver supports but not newer, and the NVIDIA_VISIBLE_DEVICES and NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES variables. Then debug: 'docker: could not select device driver with capabilities gpu', and nvidia-smi works on host but torch.cuda.is_available() is False in the container. Commands that need a GPU or daemon must be labelled not executed.",GPU),
(3,'🟡',"Our model image is 9 GB and takes 15 minutes to pull on every node. Give a prioritised plan to shrink it: slim or runtime base, multi-stage, CPU-only torch wheel when no GPU is needed, pip --no-cache-dir or a cache mount, removing build tools and apt lists in the same RUN, .dockerignore, not baking training data or checkpoints, and splitting a big model into a mounted volume. For each step say what it saves and what risk it adds. Include a Python script that reads a docker history style table and ranks layers by size.",PRA),
(3,'🔴',"Reproducible Docker builds for ML. Explain why FROM python:3.11 is not reproducible, how to pin a base image by digest (tag plus sha256), why pip install without a lockfile or hashes drifts, how apt-get install pins work and their limits, why timestamps and SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH matter for byte-identical images, and how you would record the image digest, git commit and data version of every trained model. State honestly the difference between reproducible build and repeatable behaviour. Show a Dockerfile, a requirements file with hashes (pip-compile or uv, whichever installs) and a CI check that fails on an unpinned FROM.",BK),
(3,'🟡',"Secrets in Docker builds and runtime. A Dockerfile has ARG PIP_TOKEN and uses it to install from a private index, and the token shows in docker history. Explain why ARG and ENV leak into image metadata and layers, how RUN --mount=type=secret keeps a secret out of the final image, why a later RUN rm does not help, how runtime secrets should be passed (env at run time, mounted files, a secret manager), and why .env must be in .dockerignore. Show the fixed Dockerfile and a script that scans a Dockerfile for suspicious ARG or ENV names.",BK),
(3,'🟡',"Hardening a model-serving container. List what you change in a Dockerfile and run command to make a container safer: a non-root USER with a fixed UID, minimal base, no secrets in layers, --read-only with tmpfs for scratch, --cap-drop ALL, --security-opt no-new-privileges, pinned base, image scan in CI, no docker.sock mount, and not running with --privileged. Explain what each protects against, one thing that breaks under a read-only filesystem for an ML app (model cache, /tmp, matplotlib or HF cache dirs), and how you fix it.",GEN),
(3,'🟡',"CMD versus ENTRYPOINT, exec form versus shell form, and PID 1. Explain how they combine, how runtime arguments override CMD and --entrypoint overrides ENTRYPOINT, why the shell form makes sh PID 1 so SIGTERM never reaches uvicorn or gunicorn, how docker stop works (SIGTERM then SIGKILL after a grace period) and how that affects in-flight predictions and checkpoint saving, and when you need tini or --init. Show a Dockerfile and a small Python signal handler test you can run locally without a daemon.",GEN),
(3,'🟡',"HEALTHCHECK for a model server. A container is 'running' but the model is still loading for 90 seconds and requests fail. Explain the Dockerfile HEALTHCHECK options (interval, timeout, start-period, retries), the difference between liveness (process alive) and readiness (model loaded and able to predict), a health endpoint that checks the model object, why curl may be missing in a slim image (use python or a tiny script), and how Compose depends_on with condition: service_healthy uses it. Say what Kubernetes probes replace (only a pointer; Kubernetes is a later part). Validate the compose file by script.",GEN),
(3,'🟡',"Volumes, bind mounts and tmpfs for ML containers. Where do you put training data, checkpoints, the model registry download cache and logs, and why? Compare named volumes, bind mounts (host path, UID and permission issues, slow on Docker Desktop) and tmpfs, what survives docker rm, the -v versus --mount syntax, how to back up a volume, and the read-only mount for input data. Show a run command that trains with data mounted read-only, checkpoints in a named volume, and a non-root user that can write there.",GEN),
(3,'🟡',"A docker-compose.yml for a local ML stack: an inference API, an MLflow tracking server, Postgres for its backend store and MinIO for artifacts. Write the file with named volumes, a user-defined network, environment via an env_file, healthchecks and depends_on conditions, and ports published only where needed. Explain how services find each other by name, why localhost inside a container is not the host, and validate the file with docker compose config (works without a daemon) plus a pyyaml check that every depends_on target exists.",GEN),
(3,'🟡',"Docker networking for a model API. The uvicorn app works with python app.py on the laptop but curl localhost:8000 hangs after docker run -p 8000:8000. Explain bridge, host and none networks, port publishing (-p host:container), why binding to 127.0.0.1 inside the container is the usual bug and 0.0.0.0 the fix, user-defined bridge DNS versus the default bridge, how a container reaches a database on the host (host.docker.internal on Docker Desktop, host-gateway on Linux), and how EXPOSE differs from -p. Show a Python check that parses a Dockerfile and compose file for the common mistakes.",GEN),
(3,'🔴',"Where do model weights live: baked into the image, mounted as a volume, or downloaded at start? Compare the three for a 2 GB model and for a 30 GB model on image size, pull time, cold start, rollbacks, registry cost, reproducibility (image digest pins the model), air-gapped clusters, and secrets for the model store. Say which you choose for a small sklearn model, a medium transformer and a big LLM, and how a startup download should be made safe (checksum, retries, a readiness gate).",PRA),
(3,'🟢',".dockerignore and the build context. docker build sends the whole directory to the daemon; a repo with data/ (20 GB), .git, models/ and notebooks makes every build slow, and COPY . leaks .env into the image. Explain the build context, what .dockerignore does and its pattern rules (including ! exceptions), why it also helps the cache, and how to check what would be sent without a daemon. Write a .dockerignore for an ML repo and a Python script that lists which files it would exclude (use a simplified matcher and say what it does not cover).",GEN),
